## PROMPT 1 — Module 1 · Shared Segmentation

```
Build the /segmentation page. Its job is to prove that five functions
independently define "priority" differently, and that a single enterprise
definition can reconcile them.

The DISAGREEMENT is the hero visual, not the model output.

SECTION A — "Five functions, five definitions of priority"
Render five vertical ranked lists side by side, one per function from
data.functions, each headed by the function short name and, in small muted
text, what it optimises for:
  Sales     — Rx potential, writer status
  Brand     — competitive switch risk
  Medical   — scientific influence
  Access    — payer mix, fulfilment risk
  Digital   — engagement propensity
Each list shows the top 12 accounts by that function's rank, as compact rows:
rank number (muted, serif), account name, score chip.
Each column's top bar uses that function's colour.

CRITICAL INTERACTION: hovering any account row highlights that SAME account in
all five columns simultaneously, with a subtle amber outline, and draws thin
connector lines between the highlighted rows across columns. This is the moment
the demo lands — the reviewer sees one account sitting at wildly different ranks.
Default the highlight to the hero account (meta.heroAccountId) on page load so
the divergence is visible without interaction.

Above the columns show a single stat line:
  "Average rank spread across functions: X positions"  (compute from rankSpread)
  and "Widest disagreement: <account name>, ranks N to M"

SECTION B — "One enterprise definition"
A single wide card. Left: the enterprise ranked list (top 12 by enterpriseRank),
same row treatment, navy top bar. Right: for the currently highlighted account,
show a horizontal stacked contribution bar built from featureContributions,
labelled with each feature and its weight. Beneath it, a plain-language line:
"Tier N · capacity ceiling M touches per month".

Include a small "Model detail" disclosure link that expands to show the five
feature weights and a one-line note: "Gradient-boosted ranker, monthly refresh,
point-in-time correct features." Collapsed by default — a data science reviewer
should be able to find it; an executive should not have to look at it.

SECTION C — the handoff
A slim footer band, amber, reading:
"PUBLISHED AS A GOVERNED DATA PRODUCT → enterprise account tier + capacity
ceiling consumed by the decision service"
with a right-aligned button "Carry forward to plan →" routing to /plan.

Human-in-the-loop detail: show a small badge on Section B reading
"Tier boundaries require council approval — v1.4.0 approved 01 Sep".
Do not present segmentation as fully autonomous.
