## PROMPT 3 — Module 3 · Arbitration

```
Build the /arbitration page. This is the centrepiece. Its job is to show the
moment of decision: five claims, three slots, and a system that decides,
suppresses and explains.

Structure it as four sequential beats, advanced by a single primary button
("Next" / "Run arbitration" / "Continue"). Only one beat is emphasised at a
time; previous beats collapse to a compact summary row rather than disappearing.

BEAT 1 — "Six claims arrived for this account"
Render all claims for the hero account as cards in a 3x2 grid. Each card:
  function chip (coloured) · brand · title · objective ·
  requested window · asserted value as a prominent number ·
  "why" line from valueBasis · evidence bullets (max 2) ·
  dependency note if dependsOn is non-empty ("Requires: {claim title}")
Every card must read as individually defensible. Do not visually rank them yet.
Caption beneath: "Each of these is correct in isolation."

BEAT 2 — "The ceiling"
Dim the claim grid. Bring forward a single centred statement:
  "Enterprise tier 2 · 3 commercial touches available this month"
with the capacity meter showing 3 empty segments, and beneath it in muted text
"Six claims. Three slots. Something has to lose."
Let this beat be deliberately sparse. The arithmetic does the persuading.

BEAT 3 — "Arbitration"
Run the cascade visibly, applying data.policy in order:
  (a) hard constraints evaluated as GATES — show each constraint id and name
      passing or blocking, as a compact checklist that resolves over ~2 seconds
  (b) tie-break rules applied in priority order — as each fires, the relevant
      claim card animates to its resolved position
Then settle into the resolved plan table (same visual language as /plan):
  3 scheduled with sequence numbers, 1 routed to firewall track, 2 suppressed.
EVERY resolved line must display its reasonCode badge (e.g. "TB-01") and the
full plain-English reason from data.arbitration.decisions. The reason text is
the most important content on this screen — give it room, do not truncate it,
do not put it behind a tooltip.

BEAT 4 — "Override"
Show the enterprise owner control. Implement data.overrideScenario:
reinstating CLM-4405 (Arvexa) must visibly DISPLACE CLM-4403, because the
ceiling is fixed at 3. Require a free-text reason before the override commits —
the button stays disabled until text is entered. Pre-fill a suggested reason
from overrideScenario.exampleReason but allow editing.
On commit:
  - the table re-sequences
  - the capacity ledger updates
  - a Decision Record entry appears: record id, timestamp, policy version,
    actor, action, reason, displaced claim
Show the decision record in a right-hand panel that has been accumulating
system decisions throughout beats 3 and 4, so the override appends to an
existing audit trail rather than creating one.

"WHY DID I LOSE?" VIEW
On each suppressed row, a small link "Why did this lose?" opens a side panel
showing: the winning claim, the rule that governed, the rule's rationale text,
the capacity position at the time, and a "Contest for next cycle" button that
logs an entry and shows a confirmation. This single panel does more for
adoption credibility than anything else on the screen — build it properly.
```

